// 2014 GS1 (Prelims) Set A — Transcribed from "E:\UPSC question paper\2014\2014 prilims ques & ans.docx"
// Contains question, choices (A, B, C, D), answer, explanation, and upsc review points
export const paper = { year: 2014, name: 'GS1', set: 'A' };

export const questions = [
{"no":1,"text":"What are the significances of a practical approach to sugarcane production known as 'Sustainable Sugarcane Initiative'?\n1. Seed cost is very low compared to the conventional method of cultivation.\n2. Drip irrigation can be practiced very effectively.\n3. There is no application of chemical/inorganic fertilizers at all.\n4. The scope for intercropping is more compared to conventional cultivation.","A":"1 and 3 only","B":"1, 2 and 4 only","C":"2, 3 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Sustainable Sugarcane Initiative","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Sustainable Sugarcane Initiative promotes efficient and sustainable sugarcane cultivation.\nIt can reduce seed costs compared with conventional planting.\nDrip irrigation can be effectively used.\nIt provides greater scope for intercropping.\nThe method does not imply complete elimination of chemical/inorganic fertilizers.","revisionPoints":"SSI → Sustainable Sugarcane Initiative\nFocus → efficient use of seed, water and land.\nDrip irrigation improves water-use efficiency.\nIntercropping can improve land utilisation.\nDo not interpret \"sustainable\" as meaning zero chemical fertilizer use."},
{"no":2,"text":"If a wetland of international importance is brought under the 'Montreux Record', what does it imply?","A":"Changes in ecological character have occurred, are occurring or are likely to occur in the wetland as a result of human interference.","B":"The country should prohibit all human activity within five kilometres of the wetland.","C":"The survival of the wetland depends on cultural practices of nearby communities.","D":"It is given the status of a World Heritage Site.","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"International Environmental Bodies","tags":["Ramsar Convention","Montreux Record","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"The Montreux Record is associated with wetlands included under the Ramsar Convention.\nIt identifies Ramsar sites where ecological character has changed, is changing, or is likely to change.\nSuch changes may result from human interference.","revisionPoints":"Montreux Record → Ramsar Convention\nConcern → ecological character of wetlands.\nIt is not equivalent to World Heritage Site status.\nIt does not automatically prohibit all human activity around a wetland."},
{"no":3,"text":"Which one of the following pairs of islands is separated from each other by the 'Ten Degree Channel'?","A":"Andaman and Nicobar","B":"Nicobar and Sumatra","C":"Maldives and Lakshadweep","D":"Sumatra and Java","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Ten Degree Channel","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"The Ten Degree Channel separates the Andaman Islands from the Nicobar Islands.\nIt is an important geographical feature of the Andaman and Nicobar archipelago.","revisionPoints":"Ten Degree Channel → Andaman & Nicobar\nLocated approximately along 10° N latitude.\nAndaman Islands lie to the north.\nNicobar Islands lie to the south."},
{"no":4,"text":"Consider the following pairs:\nProgramme/Project\nMinistry\n1. Drought-Prone Area Programme\nMinistry of Agriculture\n2. Desert Development Programme\nMinistry of Environment and Forests\n3. National Watershed Development Project for Rainfed Areas\nMinistry of Rural Development\nWhich of the above pairs is/are correctly matched?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"3 only","C":"1, 2 and 3","D":"None","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["Watershed Development","Drought-Prone Area Programme","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"According to the source, none of the three pairs is correctly matched.\nThe question tests knowledge of the ministries responsible for various rural-development and watershed programmes.\nTherefore, all three proposed ministry-programme combinations are incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Drought-Prone Area Programme → area development\nDesert Development Programme → dryland/desert development\nWatershed development → soil and water conservation\nIn such questions, carefully distinguish the programme objective from the ministry administering it."},
{"no":5,"text":"With reference to Bombay Natural History Society (BNHS), consider the following statements:\n1. It is an autonomous organization under the Ministry of Environment and Forests.\n2. It strives to conserve nature through action-based research, education and public awareness.\n3. It organizes and conducts nature trails and camps for the general public.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 and 3 only","B":"2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Bombay Natural History Society","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"BNHS works towards nature conservation through research, education and public awareness.\nIt also conducts nature trails and camps.\nStatement 1 is incorrect because BNHS is not an autonomous organisation under the Ministry of Environment and Forests in the manner stated.","revisionPoints":"BNHS → nature conservation organisation\nActivities include:\nResearch\nEducation\nPublic awareness\nNature activities\nImportant organisation for Indian biodiversity and wildlife conservation."},
{"no":6,"text":"With reference to Global Environment Facility (GEF), which of the following statements is/are correct?","A":"It serves as the financial mechanism for the Convention on Biological Diversity and the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change.","B":"It undertakes scientific research on environmental issues at the global level.","C":"It is an agency under OECD to facilitate transfer of technology and funds to underdeveloped countries.","D":"Both (a) and (b)","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"International Environmental Bodies","tags":["Global Environment Facility","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"The GEF serves as a financial mechanism for major international environmental conventions.\nThis includes the Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD) and UNFCCC.\nIt is not primarily a global scientific research organisation.\nIt is also not an agency under the OECD.","revisionPoints":"GEF → Global Environment Facility\nProvides environmental financing.\nAssociated with:\nBiodiversity\nClimate change\nInternational environmental conventions\nDistinguish a financial mechanism from a scientific research body."},
{"no":7,"text":"With reference to technologies for solar power production, consider the following statements:\n1. Photovoltaics generates electricity by direct conversion of light into electricity, while Solar Thermal uses the Sun's rays to generate heat which is further used in electricity generation.\n2. Photovoltaics generates AC, while Solar Thermal generates DC.\n3. India has a manufacturing base for Solar Thermal technology, but not for Photovoltaics.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1, 2 and 3","D":"None","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Renewable Energy","tags":["Photovoltaics","Solar Thermal","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Photovoltaic technology directly converts sunlight into electrical energy.\nSolar thermal technology first converts solar radiation into heat, which can then be used to generate electricity.\nStatement 2 is incorrect because photovoltaic systems produce DC electricity initially, which can be converted to AC.\nStatement 3 is also incorrect.","revisionPoints":"PV → sunlight directly to electricity\nSolar thermal → sunlight → heat → electricity\nPV cells initially produce DC.\nInverters can convert DC into AC for grid use."},
{"no":8,"text":"Consider the following languages:\n1. Gujarati\n2. Kannada\n3. Telugu\nWhich of the above has/have been declared as Classical Languages by the Government?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"3 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Literature","tags":["Classical Languages","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Kannada and Telugu have been recognised as Classical Languages.\nGujarati was not included in the classical languages listed in the source.\nClassical-language recognition is associated with antiquity and a rich independent literary tradition.","revisionPoints":"Kannada → Classical Language\nTelugu → Classical Language\nGujarati → not included in the source's classical-language list.\nClassical-language status is based on criteria related to antiquity and literary heritage."},
{"no":9,"text":"Consider the following pairs:\n1. Dampa Tiger Reserve : Mizoram\n2. Gumti Wildlife Sanctuary : Sikkim\n3. Saramati Peak : Nagaland\nWhich of the above pairs is/are correctly matched?","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Dampa Tiger Reserve","Gumti Wildlife Sanctuary","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Dampa Tiger Reserve is located in Mizoram.\nSaramati Peak is associated with Nagaland.\nGumti Wildlife Sanctuary is not located in Sikkim.","revisionPoints":"Dampa Tiger Reserve → Mizoram\nSaramati Peak → Nagaland\nSaramati is one of the prominent peaks of northeastern India.\nLocation-based questions often combine protected areas, mountains and States."},
{"no":10,"text":"With reference to a conservation organization called Wetlands International, which of the following statements is/are correct?\n1. It is an intergovernmental organization formed by countries that are signatories to the Ramsar Convention.\n2. It works at the field level to develop and mobilize knowledge, and uses practical experience to advocate for better policies.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"International Environmental Bodies","tags":["Wetlands International","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Wetlands International works on wetland conservation and sustainable management.\nIt works at field level and uses practical knowledge to support better policies.\nIt is not an intergovernmental organisation formed by Ramsar signatories.","revisionPoints":"Wetlands International → wetland conservation\nWorks through:\nKnowledge generation\nField-level activities\nPolicy advocacy\nDo not confuse it with the Ramsar Convention, which is an international treaty."},
{"no":11,"text":"With reference to the grouping of countries known as BRICS, consider the following statements:\n1. The First Summit of BRICS was held in Rio de Janeiro in 2009.\n2. South Africa was the last to join the BRICS grouping.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Regional Organizations","tags":["BRICS","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"The first BRIC summit was held in Yekaterinburg, Russia, in 2009, not Rio de Janeiro.\nSouth Africa joined the grouping later, making it BRICS.\nTherefore, only statement 2 is correct according to the source.","revisionPoints":"BRIC → Brazil, Russia, India, China\nSouth Africa joined → BRICS.\nFirst BRIC Summit → 2009, Yekaterinburg.\nBRICS represents cooperation among major emerging economies."},
{"no":12,"text":"Consider the following diseases:\n1. Diphtheria\n2. Chickenpox\n3. Smallpox\nWhich of the above diseases has/have been eradicated in India?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"3 only","C":"1, 2 and 3","D":"None","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Disease Eradication","Smallpox","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Smallpox has been eradicated globally and therefore in India.\nDiphtheria and chickenpox have not been eradicated.\nVaccination and public-health programmes play important roles in controlling infectious diseases.","revisionPoints":"Smallpox → eradicated\nSmallpox eradication was achieved through a global vaccination campaign.\nEradication means elimination of disease transmission worldwide.\nDo not confuse eradication with control or elimination from a particular region."},
{"no":13,"text":"Which of the following phenomena might have influenced the evolution of organisms?\n1. Continental drift\n2. Glacial cycles","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Evolution","Continental Drift","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Continental drift changes the geographical distribution and isolation of species.\nGeographic isolation can contribute to divergence and speciation.\nGlacial cycles cause major changes in climate and habitats.\nBoth phenomena can therefore influence evolutionary processes.","revisionPoints":"Continental drift → geographical isolation\nGlacial cycles → climatic and habitat changes\nEnvironmental changes can exert selection pressure.\nGeographic isolation can contribute to speciation."},
{"no":14,"text":"Other than poaching, what are the possible reasons for the decline in the population of Ganges River Dolphins?\n1. Construction of dams and barrages on rivers\n2. Increase in the population of crocodiles in rivers\n3. Getting trapped in fishing nets accidentally\n4. Use of synthetic fertilizers and other agricultural chemicals in crop-fields near rivers","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1, 3 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Ganges River Dolphin","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Dams and barrages alter river flow and habitat conditions.\nDolphins can become accidentally trapped in fishing nets.\nAgricultural chemicals can enter rivers and contribute to pollution.\nIncrease in crocodile population is not identified as a major cause in the source.","revisionPoints":"Ganges River Dolphin → freshwater aquatic mammal\nMajor pressures include:\nHabitat fragmentation\nPollution\nFishing-related mortality\nAltered river flow\nRiver connectivity is important for aquatic biodiversity."},
{"no":15,"text":"The Radcliffe Committee was appointed to","A":"Solve the problem of minorities in India","B":"Give effect to the Independence Bill","C":"Delimit the boundaries between India and Pakistan","D":"Enquire into the riots in East Bengal","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Radcliffe Committee","Partition of India","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"The Radcliffe Commission was established during the partition of British India.\nIts task was to determine the boundaries between the newly created India and Pakistan.\nThe boundary decisions affected Punjab and Bengal.","revisionPoints":"Radcliffe Commission → Partition boundary\nChaired by Sir Cyril Radcliffe.\nAssociated with the 1947 Partition.\nSeparate boundary arrangements were made for Punjab and Bengal."},
{"no":16,"text":"Brominated flame retardants are used in many household products like mattresses and upholstery. Why is there some concern about their use?\n1. They are highly resistant to degradation in the environment.\n2. They are able to accumulate in humans and animals.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Pollution","tags":["Brominated Flame Retardants","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Some brominated flame retardants are persistent environmental pollutants.\nTheir resistance to degradation can allow them to remain in the environment.\nThey can also bioaccumulate in organisms.\nThese properties create environmental and health concerns.","revisionPoints":"Persistent → resistant to environmental degradation\nBioaccumulation → build-up within an organism\nPersistent chemicals may remain in ecosystems for long periods.\nSome pollutants can enter food chains and undergo biomagnification."},
{"no":17,"text":"Consider the following:\n1. Bats\n2. Bears\n3. Rodents\nThe phenomenon of hibernation can be observed in which of the above?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 only","C":"1, 2 and 3","D":"Hibernation cannot be observed in any of the above","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Hibernation","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Hibernation is a state of greatly reduced metabolic activity.\nIt helps animals survive periods of low temperature or limited food availability.\nHibernation or hibernation-like states can be observed in bats, bears and several rodents.","revisionPoints":"Hibernation → reduced metabolic activity\nBody temperature and metabolic rate may decrease.\nHelps conserve energy during unfavourable conditions.\nDifferent species show different forms and durations of dormancy."},
{"no":18,"text":"Which one of the following is the largest Committee of Parliament?","A":"Committee on Public Accounts","B":"Committee on Estimates","C":"Committee on Public Undertakings","D":"Committee on Petitions","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["Committee on Estimates","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"The Estimates Committee is the largest financial committee of Parliament.\nIt examines estimates of expenditure and suggests economies and improvements in administration.\nIt consists of members of the Lok Sabha.","revisionPoints":"Estimates Committee → largest financial committee\nExamines expenditure estimates.\nConsists of 30 members from Lok Sabha.\nPublic Accounts Committee → 22 members.\nCommittee on Public Undertakings → examines public-sector undertakings."},
{"no":19,"text":"Which of the following adds/add carbon dioxide to the carbon cycle on planet Earth?\n1. Volcanic action\n2. Respiration\n3. Photosynthesis\n4. Decay of organic matter","A":"1 and 3 only","B":"2 only","C":"1, 2 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Carbon Cycle","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Volcanic activity releases carbon dioxide into the atmosphere.\nRespiration releases CO₂.\nDecay of organic matter also releases carbon dioxide.\nPhotosynthesis consumes CO₂ rather than adding it to the atmosphere.","revisionPoints":"Photosynthesis → removes atmospheric CO₂\nRespiration → releases CO₂\nDecomposition → releases carbon\nVolcanic activity is a natural geological source of atmospheric carbon dioxide."},
{"no":20,"text":"If you walk through countryside, you are likely to see some birds stalking alongside cattle to seize insects disturbed by their movement through grasses. Which of the following is/are such bird/birds?\n1. Painted Stork\n2. Common Myna\n3. Black-necked Crane","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 only","C":"2 and 3","D":"3 only","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Cattle Egret","Common Myna","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Common Myna is known to forage around grazing cattle and capture insects disturbed by their movement.\nThis is an example of a feeding association between animals.\nPainted Stork and Black-necked Crane do not fit the behaviour described in the question.","revisionPoints":"Common Myna → often associated with grazing livestock\nIt feeds on insects disturbed from grass and soil.\nSuch associations demonstrate different foraging strategies.\nBehavioural ecology examines relationships between organisms and their environment."},
{"no":21,"text":"In ancient India, the terms 'Mahattara' and 'Pattakila' referred to:","A":"Military officers","B":"Village headmen","C":"Judges","D":"Religious teachers","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Mahattara","Pattakila","Village Headmen","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Mahattara and Pattakila were associated with local village administration.\nThey functioned as village headmen/local administrative authorities.\nTheir presence reflects the importance of local administrative institutions in ancient India.","revisionPoints":"Mahattara → village headman\nPattakila → village headman\nAncient Indian administration had several levels of local authority.\nVillage administration was important for revenue and local affairs."},
{"no":22,"text":"Lichens are well known as a symbiotic association between:","A":"Algae and bacteria","B":"Algae and fungi","C":"Fungi and bacteria","D":"Fungi and mosses","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Lichens","Symbiosis","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"A lichen consists of a fungal component and a photosynthetic partner, commonly an alga.\nThe photosynthetic partner provides organic nutrients through photosynthesis.\nThe fungus provides shelter, water and minerals.","revisionPoints":"Lichen = fungus + photosynthetic partner\nCommonly described as algae + fungi.\nLichens can survive in harsh environments.\nThey are also useful as indicators of air pollution."},
{"no":23,"text":"Which of the following pairs is/are correctly matched?\n1. Oak and Rhododendron — Himalayas\n2. Sandalwood — Himalayas","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Himalayan Flora","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Oak and Rhododendron are naturally associated with the Himalayan region.\nSandalwood is not naturally characteristic of the Himalayas.\nVegetation distribution depends strongly on altitude, temperature and rainfall.","revisionPoints":"Oak → Himalayan vegetation\nRhododendron → Himalayan region\nVegetation changes with altitude and climatic conditions.\nSandalwood is associated with warmer regions of India."},
{"no":24,"text":"Which of the following pollutants are released by the steel industry?\n1. Sulphur oxides\n2. Nitrogen oxides\n3. Carbon monoxide\n4. Carbon dioxide","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1, 2 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Pollution","tags":["Steel Industry Pollutants","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Steel production involves combustion and high-temperature industrial processes.\nThese can release SOx, NOx, CO and CO₂.\nThe exact quantity depends on the process, fuel and pollution-control technology.","revisionPoints":"Steel industry → significant industrial emission source.\nSOx → air pollution\nNOx → air pollution and secondary pollutants\nCO₂ → greenhouse gas\nCleaner technologies can reduce industrial emissions."},
{"no":25,"text":"Which of the following kingdoms were associated with the life of Buddha?\n1. Avanti\n2. Kosala\n3. Magadha\n4. Vatsa","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Buddha","Mahajanapadas","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Kosala and Magadha were important kingdoms during the lifetime of Buddha.\nBoth played major roles in the political and religious environment of the 6th–5th centuries BCE.\nBuddhist traditions contain several references to these kingdoms and their rulers.","revisionPoints":"Kosala → important Mahajanapada\nMagadha → major Mahajanapada\nBuddha's life is closely associated with the political landscape of northern India.\nMagadha later became the centre of powerful empires."},
{"no":26,"text":"The month-long festival/campaign involving planting of saplings is associated with which communities?","A":"Bhil and Sahariya","B":"Gond and Korku","C":"Toda and Irula","D":"Khasi and Garo","ans":"B","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Festivals & Traditions","tags":["Gond","Korku","Tree Planting Festival","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"The source associates the month-long sapling-planting campaign with the Gond and Korku communities.\nSuch community-based environmental practices combine traditional social participation with conservation.","revisionPoints":"Gond and Korku → central Indian tribal communities\nTraditional communities often have strong links with forests.\nCommunity participation can support afforestation and biodiversity conservation.\nTribal ecological practices are important for sustainable resource management."},
{"no":27,"text":"The tax on toothpaste is an example of:","A":"Tax imposed by Centre and collected by Centre","B":"Tax imposed by State and collected by Centre","C":"Tax imposed by Centre and collected by State","D":"Tax imposed by State and collected by State","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Taxation","tags":["Sales Tax","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"According to the source framing, sales tax on toothpaste is treated as a State-imposed and State-collected tax.\nThe question tests the distinction between taxation powers and collection mechanisms.","revisionPoints":"Historically, sales tax was primarily associated with States.\nTax assignment between Centre and States is an important feature of Indian federal finance.\nGST later subsumed many indirect taxes, including State sales taxes on many goods.\nAlways consider the time period in taxation questions."},
{"no":28,"text":"Which one of the following best describes Venture Capital?","A":"A short-term investment in government securities","B":"Long-term start-up capital provided to new entrepreneurs","C":"Loans provided by commercial banks to industries","D":"Financial assistance given by government to public-sector enterprises","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Financial Markets","tags":["Venture Capital","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Venture capital provides funding to new or high-growth businesses, often involving substantial risk.\nInvestors generally expect high growth in return for accepting higher risk.\nIt is particularly important for innovative start-ups.","revisionPoints":"Venture capital → start-up/high-growth financing\nUsually associated with equity investment.\nHigher risk → potentially higher return.\nDifferent from conventional bank loans."},
{"no":29,"text":"What was the main objective of the 12th Five-Year Plan?","A":"Faster, more inclusive and sustainable growth","B":"Faster economic growth with complete privatization","C":"Sustainable and inclusive growth to reduce unemployment","D":"Rapid industrialisation through public-sector expansion","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["12th Five-Year Plan","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"The source identifies sustainable and inclusive growth and reduction of unemployment as the main objective.\nThe plan focused on balancing economic growth with inclusion and sustainability.","revisionPoints":"12th Five-Year Plan → 2012–2017\nEmphasis on inclusive growth.\nSustainability was an important planning concern.\nEmployment generation was an important developmental objective."},
{"no":30,"text":"The Current Account of an economy consists of:","A":"Balance of trade only","B":"Balance of trade + capital account","C":"Balance of trade + balance of invisibles","D":"Balance of payments + capital account","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Balance of Payments","Current Account","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"The current account records transactions involving goods, services, income and transfers.\nTraditionally, it can be understood as balance of trade + balance of invisibles.\nInvisibles include services, income and transfers.","revisionPoints":"Current Account → goods + services + income + transfers\nBalance of Trade → exports − imports of goods\nInvisibles include services and transfers.\nCapital/financial transactions are recorded separately."},
{"no":31,"text":"MSF Rate and NDTL are terms associated with:","A":"Banking operations","B":"Stock market operations","C":"Foreign exchange markets","D":"Government budgeting","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["Marginal Standing Facility","NDTL","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"MSF stands for Marginal Standing Facility.\nIt is a facility through which scheduled commercial banks can obtain overnight funds from the RBI under specified conditions.\nNDTL means Net Demand and Time Liabilities, an important banking-liability measure.","revisionPoints":"MSF → RBI liquidity facility\nNDTL → Net Demand and Time Liabilities\nBoth are connected with the banking system.\nRBI uses monetary-policy instruments to influence liquidity and credit conditions."},
{"no":32,"text":"What is the role of Business Correspondents (BCs) in banking?\n1. They enable banks to provide services in areas where there are no branches.\n2. They can facilitate deposits and withdrawals for customers.\nWhich of the above is/are correct according to the source?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["Business Correspondents","Bank Saathi","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Business Correspondents act as intermediaries that help banks extend services to customers.\nThey can facilitate transactions such as deposits and withdrawals.\nThe source treats statement 1 as incorrect in the given framing.","revisionPoints":"BC model → financial inclusion\nHelps extend banking services beyond conventional branches.\nCan facilitate basic banking transactions.\nParticularly useful in underserved and rural areas."},
{"no":33,"text":"Statutory Reserve Requirements imposed on banks serve which purpose?","A":"Enable the Central Bank to control the advances that banks can create","B":"Ensure banks have sufficient funds to meet all depositor demands","C":"Prevent banks from earning excessive profits","D":"Ensure equal distribution of credit among all sectors","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["Statutory Reserve Requirements","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Reserve requirements influence the amount of funds banks can use for lending.\nTherefore, they can help the central bank regulate credit creation.\nThey are an important part of monetary and banking regulation.","revisionPoints":"Reserve requirements affect bank liquidity.\nThey influence the capacity of banks to create credit.\nRBI uses monetary tools to manage liquidity and credit conditions.\nDo not confuse reserve requirements with profitability controls."},
{"no":34,"text":"The Arab Spring began in:","A":"Egypt","B":"Tunisia","C":"Libya","D":"Syria","ans":"B","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Countries in News","tags":["Arab Spring","Tunisia","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"The Arab Spring began in Tunisia in late 2010.\nProtests subsequently spread to several countries in North Africa and West Asia.\nThe movement involved demands related to political, economic and social conditions.","revisionPoints":"Arab Spring → began in Tunisia\nBegan around 2010–11.\nSpread to countries including Egypt, Libya, Yemen and Syria.\nIt had different political outcomes in different countries."},
{"no":35,"text":"Which of the following countries are members of the Arctic Council?\n1. Denmark\n2. Japan\n3. Russian Federation","A":"1 and 3 only","B":"2 only","C":"1, 2 and 3","D":"3 only","ans":"A","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Regional Organizations","tags":["Arctic Council","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Denmark and the Russian Federation are Arctic Council member states.\nJapan is not a member state; it has observer status.\nThe Arctic Council is an intergovernmental forum focused on Arctic cooperation.","revisionPoints":"Arctic Council has 8 Arctic States as members.\nMembers include:\nCanada\nDenmark\nFinland\nIceland\nNorway\nRussia\nSweden\nUnited States\nJapan → Observer, not member."},
{"no":36,"text":"Consider the following pairs:\n1. Chechnya — Russian Federation\n2. Darfur — Mali\n3. Swat Valley — Iraq\nWhich of the pairs given above is/are correctly matched?","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Countries in News","tags":["Chechnya","Darfur","Swat Valley","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Chechnya is a republic within the Russian Federation.\nDarfur is a region of Sudan, not Mali.\nSwat Valley is in Pakistan, not Iraq.","revisionPoints":"Chechnya → Russia\nDarfur → Sudan\nSwat Valley → Pakistan\nLocation questions often test regions associated with conflict and political geography."},
{"no":37,"text":"With reference to Agni-IV missile, which of the following statements is/are correct?\n1. It is a surface-to-surface missile.\n2. It uses only liquid propellant.\n3. It can carry a one-tonne warhead up to 7,500 km.","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1, 2 and 3","D":"None","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Defence Technology","tags":["Agni-IV Missile","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Agni-IV is a surface-to-surface ballistic missile.\nThe source does not support the claim that it uses only liquid propellant.\nThe stated range and one-tonne warhead combination in statement 3 is incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Agni series → ballistic missile family\nAgni-IV → surface-to-surface missile.\nMissile questions can test:\nRange\nPropulsion\nLaunch platform\nWarhead capability\nAvoid assuming that all Agni variants have identical characteristics."},
{"no":38,"text":"With reference to coalbed methane and shale gas, consider the following statements:\n1. Coalbed methane is extracted from coal seams.\n2. Shale gas is extracted from shale formations.\n3. Both are conventional sources of natural gas.\nWhich of the above is/are correct according to the source?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 2 only","D":"None","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Mining & Minerals","tags":["Coalbed Methane","Shale Gas","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"The source marks the proposed statements as incorrect in its framing.\nCoalbed methane and shale gas are associated with unconventional hydrocarbon resources.\nTheir extraction generally requires specialised techniques.","revisionPoints":"Coalbed methane → associated with coal seams\nShale gas → associated with shale formations\nBoth are generally classified as unconventional gas resources.\nExtraction can require advanced drilling and stimulation techniques."},
{"no":39,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding the Changpa community:\n1. They rear the Pashmina goat.\n2. They are classified as Scheduled Tribes.\n3. They are mainly found in Uttarakhand.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Festivals & Traditions","tags":["Changpa Community","Pashmina","Ladakh","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"The Changpa are traditionally associated with the rearing of Pashmina goats.\nThey are recognised as a Scheduled Tribe.\nTheir major population is associated with the Ladakh region, not Uttarakhand.","revisionPoints":"Changpa → Ladakh\nFamous for Pashmina wool.\nTraditionally pastoral.\nRecognised as a Scheduled Tribe.\nPashmina is obtained from the fine undercoat of specific goats."},
{"no":40,"text":"Guar gum, which is used in the extraction of shale gas, is obtained from:","A":"Cotton seed","B":"Guar seed","C":"Jatropha seed","D":"Groundnut seed","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Cluster Bean","Guar Gum","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Guar gum is obtained from the seeds of the guar plant.\nIt is widely used as a thickening agent.\nIn shale gas extraction, it can be used in hydraulic fracturing fluids to modify viscosity.","revisionPoints":"Guar gum → guar seed\nGuar is also known as cluster bean.\nIndia is an important producer of guar.\nGuar gum has applications in:\nFood processing\nPharmaceuticals\nOil and gas industry"},
{"no":41,"text":"Which of the following areas are known for coral reefs in India?\n1. Andaman and Nicobar Islands\n2. Gulf of Kachchh\n3. Gulf of Mannar\n4. Sundarbans","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1, 2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Coral Reefs","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Coral reefs occur around the Andaman and Nicobar Islands.\nThe Gulf of Kachchh has important coral reef ecosystems.\nThe Gulf of Mannar is another major coral reef region.\nThe Sundarbans are primarily known for their mangrove ecosystem, not coral reefs.","revisionPoints":"Major Indian coral reef areas:\nAndaman & Nicobar\nGulf of Kachchh\nGulf of Mannar\nLakshadweep\nCoral reefs generally require warm, shallow and clear marine waters.\nMangroves and coral reefs are distinct coastal ecosystems."},
{"no":42,"text":"Which of the following is/are associated with soil erosion?\n1. Deforestation\n2. Terrace cultivation\n3. Tropical climate","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Soil Erosion","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Deforestation removes vegetation cover and exposes soil to erosion.\nTerrace cultivation is generally used to reduce soil erosion on slopes rather than cause it.\nTropical climate by itself is not necessarily a cause of soil erosion.","revisionPoints":"Vegetation cover protects soil from water and wind erosion.\nDeforestation can increase surface runoff.\nTerracing reduces slope length and runoff velocity.\nSoil erosion can lead to loss of fertile topsoil."},
{"no":43,"text":"The seasonal reversal of winds is the most important characteristic of:","A":"Mediterranean climate","B":"Equatorial climate","C":"Monsoon climate","D":"Tundra climate","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Monsoon Climate","Seasonal Wind Reversal","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"A defining characteristic of the monsoon climate is the seasonal reversal of prevailing winds.\nIn South Asia, this produces distinct summer and winter monsoon patterns.\nThe reversal is associated with seasonal differences in heating and pressure.","revisionPoints":"Monsoon → seasonal reversal of winds\nSummer → generally moisture-bearing winds toward land.\nWinter → generally drier winds toward the sea.\nMonsoon influences rainfall, agriculture and water availability."},
{"no":44,"text":"The term 'Panchayatan' refers to:","A":"An assembly of village elders","B":"A system of land revenue collection","C":"A style of temple construction","D":"A type of Buddhist monastery","ans":"C","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Temples & Shrines","tags":["Panchayatan Style","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Panchayatan refers to a temple arrangement involving a principal shrine surrounded by four subsidiary shrines.\nIt is an important concept in Indian temple architecture.","revisionPoints":"Panchayatan → temple architecture\nMain shrine + four subsidiary shrines.\nAssociated with the development of Hindu temple architecture.\nArchitectural questions often test terminology and structural arrangements."},
{"no":45,"text":"Which of the following rivers flow through Arunachal Pradesh?\n1. Lohit\n2. Subansiri\n3. Barak","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Rivers of Arunachal Pradesh","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"The Lohit is an important river of Arunachal Pradesh.\nThe Subansiri also flows through Arunachal Pradesh.\nThe Barak is associated mainly with the northeastern region but does not flow through Arunachal Pradesh in the manner stated.","revisionPoints":"Lohit → Arunachal Pradesh\nSubansiri → Arunachal Pradesh\nBoth are important tributaries of the Brahmaputra system.\nRiver-location questions are important for Indian geography."},
{"no":46,"text":"Which of the following pairs is/are correctly matched?\n1. Harike Wetlands — Beas and Sutlej\n2. Kolleru Lake — Krishna and Godavari\n3. Pulicat Lake — Pennar and Cauvery","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Wetlands","River Confluence","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Harike Wetlands are formed at the confluence of the Beas and Sutlej.\nThe other river associations given in the question are incorrect.\nWetland questions often require precise knowledge of river-lake relationships.","revisionPoints":"Harike → Beas + Sutlej\nLocated in Punjab.\nIt is an important wetland and bird habitat.\nRiver confluences can create significant wetland ecosystems."},
{"no":47,"text":"Which of the following are not among the six orthodox systems of Indian philosophy?\n1. Lokayata\n2. Kapalika\n3. Sankhya\n4. Yoga","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"3 and 4 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"2 and 4 only","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Six Systems of Indian Philosophy","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Lokayata/Charvaka is a materialist philosophical tradition and is not counted among the six orthodox systems.\nKapalika is a Shaiva religious tradition, not one of the six orthodox philosophical systems.\nSankhya and Yoga are among the six orthodox systems.","revisionPoints":"The six orthodox Astika systems are:\nNyaya\nVaisheshika\nSankhya\nYoga\nMimamsa\nVedanta\nLokayata/Charvaka → heterodox\nBuddhism and Jainism → heterodox traditions"},
{"no":48,"text":"Which of the following pairs is/are correctly matched?\n1. Mahadeo Hills — Central India\n2. Mikir Hills — Northeast India\n3. Nallamala Hills — Western India","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 2 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Hill Ranges of India","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"The Mahadeo Hills are part of the central Indian highland region.\nThe Mikir Hills are located in Northeast India.\nNallamala Hills are in southern India, not western India.","revisionPoints":"Mahadeo Hills → Central India\nMikir Hills → Northeast India\nNallamala Hills → Andhra Pradesh/Telangana region\nHill-location questions require attention to regional geography."},
{"no":49,"text":"The Anti-Defection Law is contained in which Schedule of the Constitution?","A":"Seventh Schedule","B":"Ninth Schedule","C":"Tenth Schedule","D":"Twelfth Schedule","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["Tenth Schedule","Anti-Defection Law","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"The Anti-Defection Law was introduced through the 52nd Constitutional Amendment Act, 1985.\nThe provisions were incorporated into the Tenth Schedule.\nIt deals with disqualification of legislators on grounds of defection.","revisionPoints":"Anti-Defection Law → Tenth Schedule\nIntroduced by the 52nd Amendment, 1985.\nApplies to members of Parliament and State legislatures.\nDecision on disqualification is made by the presiding officer, subject to judicial review."},
{"no":50,"text":"Which of the following is the most appropriate strategy for conserving biodiversity while allowing traditional human life to continue?","A":"Biosphere Reserves","B":"National Parks","C":"Wildlife Sanctuaries","D":"Botanical Gardens","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Biosphere Reserves","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Biosphere Reserves aim to combine biodiversity conservation with sustainable human use.\nThey generally include zones with different levels of protection and human activity.\nThis makes them suitable for balancing conservation and traditional livelihoods.","revisionPoints":"Biosphere Reserve → conservation + sustainable human use\nGenerally includes:\nCore zone\nBuffer zone\nTransition zone\nNational Parks generally have stricter protection.\nBiosphere Reserves are associated with the UNESCO MAB Programme."},
{"no":51,"text":"Which country lies between the Black Sea and the Mediterranean Sea?","A":"Syria","B":"Turkey","C":"Lebanon","D":"Israel","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Turkey","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Turkey occupies a strategically important location between Europe and Asia.\nIt has coastlines on the Black Sea, Aegean Sea and Mediterranean Sea.\nThe Bosporus and Dardanelles connect the Black Sea with the Mediterranean through the Sea of Marmara.","revisionPoints":"Turkey → Black Sea + Mediterranean\nBosporus → Black Sea to Sea of Marmara\nDardanelles → Sea of Marmara to Aegean Sea\nTurkey has major geopolitical importance because of its location."},
{"no":52,"text":"Which sequence of cities is arranged from south to north?\n1. Jakarta\n2. Singapore\n3. Bangkok\n4. Hanoi","A":"Jakarta – Bangkok – Singapore – Hanoi","B":"Jakarta – Singapore – Bangkok – Hanoi","C":"Singapore – Jakarta – Hanoi – Bangkok","D":"Bangkok – Jakarta – Singapore – Hanoi","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["South-East Asian Cities","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Jakarta is the southernmost among these cities.\nSingapore lies north of Jakarta.\nBangkok lies farther north.\nHanoi is the northernmost among the four.","revisionPoints":"Jakarta → Indonesia\nSingapore → Singapore\nBangkok → Thailand\nHanoi → Vietnam\nLatitude-based ordering is a common UPSC geography technique."},
{"no":53,"text":"If global warming reaches more than 3°C above pre-industrial levels, which of the following are possible consequences?\n1. The terrestrial biosphere may become a net source of carbon instead of a net sink.\n2. Widespread mortality of coral reefs may occur.\n3. All wetlands of the world will disappear.\n4. Cereals cannot be cultivated anywhere on Earth.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1, 2 and 3","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Climate Change","tags":["Global Temperature Rise","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Higher warming can reduce the ability of terrestrial ecosystems to act as carbon sinks.\nCoral reefs are highly vulnerable to increasing temperatures and ocean warming.\nThe statements that all wetlands would disappear and cereals could not be grown anywhere are overly absolute.","revisionPoints":"Climate warming can affect:\nCarbon cycle\nCoral reefs\nWater availability\nEcosystems\nAgriculture\nCoral bleaching is strongly associated with thermal stress.\nAvoid absolute statements such as \"all\" or \"nowhere\" unless scientifically justified."},
{"no":54,"text":"The phrase \"Satyameva Jayate\" is taken from:","A":"Rig Veda","B":"Mundaka Upanishad","C":"Bhagavad Gita","D":"Mahabharata","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["National Emblem","Satyameva Jayate","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Satyameva Jayate means \"Truth alone triumphs.\"\nIt is taken from the Mundaka Upanishad.\nIt is used as the national motto of India beneath the State Emblem.","revisionPoints":"Satyameva Jayate → Mundaka Upanishad\nMeaning → Truth alone triumphs.\nAssociated with India's State Emblem.\nImportant connection between ancient Indian texts and modern constitutional symbols."},
{"no":55,"text":"Promotion of international peace and security is included in which part of the Constitution?","A":"Fundamental Rights","B":"Directive Principles of State Policy","C":"Fundamental Duties","D":"Preamble only","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["Directive Principles of State Policy","International Peace","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Article 51 directs the State to promote international peace and security.\nIt forms part of the Directive Principles of State Policy.\nDPSPs guide the State in governance and policy-making.","revisionPoints":"Article 51 → International peace and security\nLocated in Part IV.\nDPSPs are generally non-justiciable.\nThey aim to establish social, economic and political conditions conducive to welfare."},
{"no":56,"text":"Which of the following are objectives of the Integrated Watershed Development Programme?\n1. Prevention of soil runoff\n2. Rainwater harvesting and groundwater recharge\n3. Regeneration of natural vegetation\n4. Linking rivers to ensure water availability","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1, 2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Integrated Watershed Development Programme","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Watershed development focuses on managing land and water resources within a watershed.\nIt aims to reduce soil erosion and runoff.\nIt promotes rainwater harvesting, groundwater recharge and vegetation regeneration.\nRiver linking is not an objective of watershed development.","revisionPoints":"Watershed management → soil + water conservation\nMajor components:\nRunoff control\nRainwater harvesting\nGroundwater recharge\nVegetation regeneration\nFocus is generally on a drainage/watershed unit, not inter-basin river linking."},
{"no":57,"text":"Planning in India was associated with which of the following institutions?\n1. National Development Council\n2. Parliament","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Government Bodies","tags":["Finance Commission","National Development Council","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"India's planning process involved institutions such as the Planning Commission and National Development Council.\nParliament also played an important role through discussion, approval of expenditure and legislative processes.\nThe source identifies both NDC and Parliament in relation to planning.","revisionPoints":"National Development Council → planning-era institution\nPlanning Commission was established in 1950.\nFive-Year Plans were central to India's development strategy.\nPlanning was linked with both executive institutions and parliamentary processes."},
{"no":58,"text":"Which of the following are functions of the Cabinet Secretariat?\n1. Preparation of the agenda for Cabinet meetings\n2. Providing secretarial assistance to Cabinet Committees\n3. Allocation of financial resources among ministries","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 2 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Public Administration","tags":["Cabinet Secretariat","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"The Cabinet Secretariat assists in the functioning of the Cabinet and Cabinet Committees.\nIt helps prepare agendas and provides secretarial assistance.\nAllocation of financial resources is not its primary function.","revisionPoints":"Cabinet Secretariat → coordination and secretarial support\nHelps in:\nCabinet agenda preparation\nCabinet Committee support\nInter-ministerial coordination\nIt plays an important role in coordination within the Union government."},
{"no":59,"text":"What is the essential characteristic of a constitutional government?","A":"Government has unlimited powers.","B":"Government authority is restricted in the interest of individual liberty.","C":"Government is controlled entirely by the judiciary.","D":"Government is controlled by the military.","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Political Theory","tags":["Constitutional Government","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Constitutional government means that government power is exercised within constitutional limits.\nConstitutionalism places restrictions on arbitrary exercise of State power.\nProtection of individual rights and liberties is an important feature.","revisionPoints":"Constitutional government → limited government\nGovernment power is subject to constitutional rules.\nProtects individual rights and liberties.\nClosely associated with rule of law and constitutionalism."},
{"no":60,"text":"Which of the following are discretionary powers of the Governor?\n1. Reporting to the President about the failure of constitutional machinery in the State.\n2. Reserving certain Bills passed by the State Legislature for the consideration of the President.\n3. Appointing ministers without the advice of the Chief Minister.\n4. Making rules for the convenient transaction of the business of the State Government.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"1 and 3 only","C":"2 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Governor","tags":["Discretionary Powers","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"The Governor can report to the President regarding a situation where constitutional machinery in the State has failed.\nThe Governor can reserve certain Bills for the President's consideration.\nMinisters are appointed on the advice of the Chief Minister within the constitutional framework.\nMaking rules for transaction of business is not treated as a discretionary power in the source.","revisionPoints":"Governor has both constitutional/discretionary functions.\nArticle 356-related report → Governor\nCertain Bills can be reserved for President.\nGovernor normally acts with the aid and advice of the Council of Ministers."},
{"no":61,"text":"If the interest rate is lowered, what is the likely effect on investment expenditure?","A":"It will increase.","B":"It will decrease.","C":"It will remain unchanged.","D":"It will first decrease and then increase.","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Interest Rate","Investment Expenditure","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Lower interest rates reduce the cost of borrowing.\nBusinesses can borrow more cheaply for investment.\nTherefore, lower interest rates generally encourage investment expenditure.","revisionPoints":"Interest rate ↓ → Cost of borrowing ↓\nLower borrowing costs can encourage investment.\nMonetary policy influences investment through interest rates.\nHigher interest rates generally make borrowing more expensive."},
{"no":62,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding the President of India:\n1. The President makes rules for the more convenient transaction of the business of the Government of India.\n2. All executive actions of the Government of India are expressed to be taken in the name of the Prime Minister.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["Rules of Business","President","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"The President can make rules for the allocation and transaction of government business.\nExecutive actions of the Government of India are expressed in the name of the President, not the Prime Minister.\nTherefore, statement 2 is incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Article 77 → conduct of Government of India business\nExecutive action is expressed in the name of the President.\nRules are made for the convenient transaction and allocation of government business.\nThe President is the constitutional head of the Union executive."},
{"no":63,"text":"With reference to a No-Confidence Motion, consider the following statements:\n1. The Constitution of India mentions the procedure for a No-Confidence Motion.\n2. A No-Confidence Motion can be introduced only in the Lok Sabha.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["No-Confidence Motion","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"The Constitution does not specifically prescribe the procedure for a No-Confidence Motion.\nIt is a parliamentary procedure governed by the Rules of Procedure and Conduct of Business.\nIt can be moved only in the Lok Sabha, because the Council of Ministers is collectively responsible to the Lok Sabha.","revisionPoints":"No-Confidence Motion → Lok Sabha\nCouncil of Ministers is collectively responsible to Lok Sabha.\nThe procedure is governed by parliamentary rules rather than a specific constitutional provision.\nIt tests whether the government retains the confidence of the House."},
{"no":64,"text":"With reference to Neem, consider the following statements:\n1. Neem oil can be used as a pesticide.\n2. Neem has no pharmaceutical applications.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Neem Tree","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Neem oil has insecticidal and pest-control properties.\nNeem and its compounds also have various pharmaceutical and medicinal applications.\nTherefore, statement 2 is incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Neem oil → biopesticidal use\nNeem contains biologically active compounds such as azadirachtin.\nNeem has applications in:\nAgriculture\nTraditional medicine\nPersonal-care products\nNeem-based pesticides can be part of integrated pest management."},
{"no":65,"text":"What is the primary function of photosynthesis in plants?","A":"Conversion of chemical energy into light energy","B":"Conversion of light energy into stored chemical energy","C":"Conversion of heat energy into mechanical energy","D":"Conversion of chemical energy directly into heat energy","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Photosynthesis","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Photosynthesis captures light energy using pigments such as chlorophyll.\nThis energy is used to produce organic compounds.\nThe captured energy becomes stored as chemical potential energy in these compounds.","revisionPoints":"Photosynthesis → light energy → chemical energy\nOccurs mainly in chloroplasts of green plants.\nUses carbon dioxide and water.\nReleases oxygen as a major by-product of oxygenic photosynthesis."},
{"no":66,"text":"Which of the following can be used as biometric identification methods?\n1. Iris recognition\n2. Retinal recognition\n3. Voice recognition","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"IT & Computers","tags":["Biometric Identification","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Iris recognition uses the unique patterns of the iris.\nRetinal recognition uses patterns of blood vessels in the retina.\nVoice recognition uses characteristics of a person's voice.\nAll three can be used as biometric identification technologies.","revisionPoints":"Biometrics → identification/authentication using biological characteristics\nPhysiological biometrics:\nFingerprint\nIris\nRetina\nFace\nBehavioural biometrics can include voice.\nBiometric systems are used for identity verification and authentication."},
{"no":67,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding vegetative propagation:\n1. Vegetative propagation produces genetically identical plants or clones.\n2. Vegetative propagation can be practised during most parts of the year.\n3. Vegetative propagation completely eliminates the possibility of viral diseases.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biotechnology","tags":["Vegetative Propagation","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Vegetative propagation uses vegetative parts such as stems, roots or leaves.\nSince there is no sexual reproduction, offspring are generally genetically identical to the parent plant.\nIt can be practised during much of the year depending on the plant and method.\nIt does not eliminate viral diseases; viruses can be transmitted through vegetative propagation.","revisionPoints":"Vegetative propagation → asexual reproduction\nProduces genetically similar/clonal plants.\nMethods include:\nCutting\nLayering\nGrafting\nBudding\nViral pathogens can be transmitted through vegetative material."},
{"no":68,"text":"Which of the following pairs is/are correctly matched?\n1. MESSENGER — Mercury\n2. Voyager 1 and 2 — Outer Solar System\n3. Cassini-Huygens — Venus","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Space","tags":["Cassini-Huygens","Messenger","Voyager","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"MESSENGER was a NASA mission to study Mercury.\nVoyager 1 and Voyager 2 explored the outer Solar System and continued into interstellar space.\nCassini-Huygens was associated with the Saturn system, not Venus.","revisionPoints":"MESSENGER → Mercury\nVoyager → outer Solar System\nCassini-Huygens → Saturn/Titan\nSpace missions can be remembered through their target planets and scientific objectives."},
{"no":69,"text":"Which of the following pairs is/are correctly matched?\n1. Kinnaur — Areca nut\n2. Mewat — Mango\n3. Coromandel — Soybean","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"3 only","D":"None","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Regional Crop Production","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"The agricultural-region and crop combinations given do not correctly correspond according to the source.\nKinnaur is a Himalayan region rather than a major areca-nut region.\nCoromandel is associated with the southeastern coastal region, but the given soybean association is incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Kinnaur → Himachal Pradesh\nMewat → Haryana/Rajasthan region\nCoromandel Coast → southeastern India\nUPSC agriculture questions often test crop-region associations."},
{"no":70,"text":"Which of the following is a chemical change?\n1. Souring of milk\n2. Crystallization of salt from seawater\n3. Melting of ice","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Chemical Change","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Souring of milk involves chemical reactions and formation of new substances, so it is a chemical change.\nCrystallization of salt is a physical separation process.\nMelting of ice is a physical change because water remains H₂O.","revisionPoints":"Chemical change → new substance formed\nSouring of milk → chemical change.\nMelting of ice → physical change.\nCrystallization → physical change.\nPhysical changes generally do not alter the chemical identity of the substance."},
{"no":71,"text":"Which of the following falls under the original jurisdiction of the Supreme Court of India?","A":"Disputes between two private citizens","B":"Disputes between two companies","C":"Disputes between the Centre and one or more States","D":"Appeals against decisions of High Courts","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Judiciary","tags":["Original Jurisdiction","Supreme Court","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"The Supreme Court has original jurisdiction in certain disputes involving the Union and States.\nSuch disputes must involve a legal right on which the existence or extent of a legal right depends.\nOrdinary private disputes do not fall under this original jurisdiction.","revisionPoints":"Article 131 → Supreme Court original jurisdiction\nCovers certain disputes involving:\nUnion vs State(s)\nState vs State\nAppeals from High Courts fall under appellate jurisdiction.\nOriginal jurisdiction means the case can be initiated directly before the Supreme Court."},
{"no":72,"text":"Which of the following techniques are used in the creation of transgenic crops?\n1. Cytoplasmic male sterility\n2. Gene silencing\n3. Budding\n4. Grafting","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1, 2 and 3","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biotechnology","tags":["Transgenic Crops","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Gene silencing can be used in genetic engineering to suppress the expression of particular genes.\nCytoplasmic male sterility is primarily associated with hybrid seed production, not itself a technique for creating transgenic crops.\nBudding and grafting are conventional vegetative propagation techniques.","revisionPoints":"Transgenic crop → crop containing an introduced foreign gene\nGene silencing → suppresses gene expression\nGrafting and budding → vegetative propagation.\nGenetic engineering and conventional plant breeding are distinct approaches."},
{"no":73,"text":"Which of the following are uses of maize?\n1. Production of starch\n2. Production of biodiesel\n3. Production of alcoholic beverages","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Maize","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Maize is an important source of starch.\nIts oil can be used as a feedstock in the production of biofuels.\nMaize is also used in the production of alcoholic beverages.\nThus, maize has food as well as industrial applications.","revisionPoints":"Maize → food + industrial crop\nUses include:\nStarch\nAnimal feed\nOil\nBiofuel feedstock\nAlcoholic beverages\nCrop utilisation is an important part of agricultural economics."},
{"no":74,"text":"Which one of the following is a crustacean?","A":"Crab","B":"Mite","C":"Scorpion","D":"Spider","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Arthropods","Crustaceans","Arachnids","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Crabs belong to the subphylum Crustacea.\nMites, scorpions and spiders belong to the class Arachnida.\nAll are arthropods but belong to different groups.","revisionPoints":"Crab → Crustacea\nSpider → Arachnida\nScorpion → Arachnida\nMite → Arachnida\nArthropoda includes diverse groups such as insects, crustaceans and arachnids."},
{"no":75,"text":"With reference to the Supreme Court of India, Parliament can:","A":"Increase the number of judges of the Supreme Court","B":"Reduce the jurisdiction of the Supreme Court","C":"Abolish the Supreme Court","D":"Remove judges without following the constitutional procedure","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Judiciary","tags":["Supreme Court Judges","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Parliament has the power to determine the number of judges of the Supreme Court by law.\nThe sanctioned strength can therefore be increased through legislation.\nRemoval of judges requires the constitutionally prescribed procedure.","revisionPoints":"Parliament determines the number of Supreme Court judges by law.\nJudges enjoy constitutional safeguards regarding tenure.\nRemoval requires a special parliamentary procedure on grounds specified by the Constitution.\nJudicial independence is a key constitutional principle."},
{"no":76,"text":"Which of the following pairs is/are correctly matched?\n1. Chanderi — Traditional sarees and fabric\n2. Kancheepuram — Traditional sarees and fabric\n3. Bhadrachalam — Traditional sarees and fabric\n4. Karnal — Traditional sarees and fabric","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"B","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Handicrafts","tags":["Traditional Sarees","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Chanderi is famous for its traditional textiles and sarees.\nKancheepuram is renowned for Kanjeevaram silk sarees.\nThe other two places are not identified with the traditional saree/fabric traditions stated in the source.","revisionPoints":"Chanderi → Madhya Pradesh\nKancheepuram → Tamil Nadu\nKancheepuram → famous for silk sarees.\nChanderi textiles are known for their characteristic fine fabric and traditional weaving."},
{"no":77,"text":"Which of the following pairs is/are correctly matched?\n1. NH-15 — Ahmedabad–Jodhpur\n2. NH-7 — Varanasi–Kanyakumari\n3. NH-2 — Delhi–Kolkata","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"All three","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Infrastructure & Transport","tags":["National Highways","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"The source identifies NH-15 with Ahmedabad–Jodhpur.\nIt also identifies NH-7 with the Varanasi–Kanyakumari route.\nThe third pairing is not correct according to the source's classification.","revisionPoints":"National Highway questions require attention to route and numbering.\nHighway numbering has undergone changes under the newer numbering system.\nAlways consider whether a question refers to old or new NH numbering."},
{"no":78,"text":"Consider the following agreements:\n1. Nagoya Protocol\n2. Cartagena Protocol\n3. Kyoto Protocol\nWhich of the above have a bearing on biodiversity?","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"International Environmental Bodies","tags":["Biodiversity Agreements","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"The Nagoya Protocol deals with access to genetic resources and benefit sharing.\nThe Cartagena Protocol deals with biosafety and living modified organisms.\nThe Kyoto Protocol addresses climate change, which has significant implications for biodiversity and ecosystems.\nTherefore, all three have a bearing on biodiversity.","revisionPoints":"Nagoya Protocol → Access and Benefit Sharing\nCartagena Protocol → Biosafety\nKyoto Protocol → Climate change\nClimate change can significantly affect ecosystems and biodiversity."},
{"no":79,"text":"With reference to Earth Hour, which of the following statements is/are correct?\n1. It is an annual movement in which people switch off lights for one hour.\n2. It aims to raise awareness about climate change and environmental issues.\n3. It is an initiative of UNEP and UNESCO.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Climate Change","tags":["Earth Hour","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Earth Hour is an annual global movement involving switching off non-essential lights for one hour.\nIt aims to raise awareness about environmental and climate-related issues.\nIt is associated with WWF, not an initiative jointly launched by UNEP and UNESCO.","revisionPoints":"Earth Hour → annual environmental awareness movement\nUsually involves a symbolic one-hour lights-off event.\nAssociated with the World Wide Fund for Nature (WWF).\nFocuses on climate and environmental awareness."},
{"no":80,"text":"Consider the following food chain:\nDiatoms → Crustaceans → Herrings\nWhich of the following represents the correct trophic relationship?","A":"Diatoms are producers, crustaceans are primary consumers and herrings are secondary consumers.","B":"Diatoms are primary consumers, crustaceans are producers and herrings are secondary consumers.","C":"Diatoms are decomposers, crustaceans are primary consumers and herrings are producers.","D":"Diatoms are producers, crustaceans are secondary consumers and herrings are decomposers.","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Food Chain","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Diatoms are photosynthetic organisms and act as primary producers.\nCrustaceans feed on organisms such as phytoplankton and function as primary consumers in this chain.\nHerrings feed on smaller organisms including crustaceans and therefore occupy a higher trophic level.","revisionPoints":"Diatoms → producers\nCrustaceans → primary consumers\nHerrings → secondary consumers\nEnergy flows through trophic levels from producers → consumers.\nOnly a fraction of energy is transferred from one trophic level to the next."},
{"no":81,"text":"The Partition of Bengal, which was announced in 1905, was annulled in:","A":"1909","B":"1911","C":"1915","D":"1919","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Partition of Bengal 1905","Lord Curzon","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"The Partition of Bengal was announced in 1905.\nIt generated strong opposition and became an important issue in the Swadeshi movement.\nThe partition was annulled in 1911.\nThe announcement was made at the Delhi Durbar during the reign of King George V.","revisionPoints":"Partition of Bengal → 1905\nAssociated with Lord Curzon.\nAnnulment → 1911.\nThe capital of British India was shifted from Calcutta to Delhi in 1911.\nThe partition contributed to the growth of the Swadeshi and Boycott movements."},
{"no":82,"text":"At which session of the Indian National Congress was the goal of Poorna Swaraj adopted?","A":"Calcutta Session, 1928","B":"Lahore Session, 1929","C":"Karachi Session, 1931","D":"Lucknow Session, 1916","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Poorna Swaraj","1929 INC Session","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"The Lahore Session of the Indian National Congress was held in 1929.\nIt adopted Poorna Swaraj (Complete Independence) as the goal.\nThe session was presided over by Jawaharlal Nehru.\n26 January 1930 was observed as Independence Day following the declaration.","revisionPoints":"Lahore Session → 1929\nPresident → Jawaharlal Nehru\nGoal → Poorna Swaraj\n26 January 1930 → Independence Day observed\nThe demand represented a shift from Dominion Status to complete independence."},
{"no":83,"text":"With reference to Sattriya, consider the following statements:\n1. Sattriya is a combination of music, dance and drama.\n2. It was developed as a Vaishnavite tradition in Assam.\n3. It was originally performed exclusively by women.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Music","tags":["Sattriya","Assam","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Sattriya combines elements of dance, music and dramatic expression.\nIt developed in Assam as part of the Vaishnavite tradition associated with Srimanta Sankardeva.\nThe statement that it was originally performed exclusively by women is incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Sattriya → Assam\nAssociated with Vaishnavism.\nLinked with the Sattras and Bhakti tradition.\nRecognised as one of India's classical dance forms.\nSrimanta Sankardeva played a major role in its development."},
{"no":84,"text":"The first day of the Saka calendar is:","A":"21 March","B":"22 March","C":"1 April","D":"21 June","ans":"B","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Festivals & Traditions","tags":["Saka Calendar","National Calendar","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"The first day of the Saka calendar, Chaitra 1, normally falls on 22 March.\nIn a leap year, it falls on 21 March.\nThe Saka calendar is used as India's national calendar.","revisionPoints":"Saka calendar → National Calendar of India\nFirst month → Chaitra\nChaitra 1 → 22 March normally\nChaitra 1 → 21 March in a leap year\nThe Saka era begins in 78 CE."},
{"no":85,"text":"Consider the following pairs:\n1. Ajanta — Mahaparinirvana of Buddha\n2. Mamallapuram — Descent of the Ganga/Arjuna's Penance\n3. Udayagiri — Varaha image\nWhich of the above is/are correctly matched?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 2 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"UNESCO Heritage","tags":["Ajanta","Mount Abu","Mamallapuram","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Ajanta contains important Buddhist paintings and sculptures, including representations associated with Buddha's Mahaparinirvana.\nMamallapuram is famous for the monumental relief commonly known as Descent of the Ganga/Arjuna's Penance.\nUdayagiri is famous for its monumental Varaha relief.\nAll three pairs are correctly associated.","revisionPoints":"Ajanta → Buddhist caves and paintings\nMamallapuram → Pallava architecture\nDescent of the Ganga → Mamallapuram\nUdayagiri → Gupta-period rock-cut monuments\nVaraha imagery is an important feature of Udayagiri."},
{"no":86,"text":"The Ghadar Party was established primarily as:","A":"A revolutionary association of Indians abroad","B":"A social reform organisation in Bengal","C":"A peasant organisation in Punjab","D":"A political organisation founded by the British Government","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Ghadar Party","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"The Ghadar movement emerged among Indians living abroad, particularly in North America.\nIt aimed at revolutionary opposition to British rule in India.\nIts headquarters was established in San Francisco.","revisionPoints":"Ghadar movement → revolutionary nationalism\nImportant centre → San Francisco\nAssociated with Indian migrants in North America.\nAimed at ending British colonial rule through revolutionary action."},
{"no":87,"text":"Kalaripayattu is:","A":"A classical dance form of North India","B":"A traditional martial art of South India","C":"A form of Buddhist meditation","D":"A traditional style of temple architecture","ans":"B","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Festivals & Traditions","tags":["Kalaripayattu","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Kalaripayattu is a traditional martial art associated particularly with Kerala.\nIt includes physical training, combat techniques and weapon-based practices.\nIt continues as a living traditional practice.","revisionPoints":"Kalaripayattu → Kerala\nTraditional martial art.\nIncludes unarmed and weapon-based techniques.\nImportant part of India's intangible cultural heritage."},
{"no":88,"text":"Which of the following pairs is/are correctly matched?\n1. Garba — Gujarat\n2. Yakshagana — Karnataka\n3. Mohiniattam — Odisha","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 2 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Music","tags":["Garba","Mohiniattam","Yakshagana","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Garba is a traditional dance associated with Gujarat.\nYakshagana is a traditional theatre and dance form associated with Karnataka.\nMohiniattam is associated with Kerala, not Odisha.","revisionPoints":"Garba → Gujarat\nYakshagana → Karnataka\nMohiniattam → Kerala\nState–dance associations are frequently tested in UPSC Art & Culture questions."},
{"no":89,"text":"Which of the following pairs is/are correctly matched?\n1. Tabo Monastery — Spiti Valley\n2. Alchi Monastery — Ladakh\n3. Lhotsava Lhakhang — Zanskar","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Buddhist Monasteries","Ladakh","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Tabo Monastery is located in the Spiti Valley of Himachal Pradesh.\nAlchi Monastery is located in Ladakh.\nThe source identifies Lhotsava Lhakhang at Nako in Kinnaur, not Zanskar.","revisionPoints":"Tabo → Spiti Valley\nAlchi → Ladakh\nNako → Kinnaur\nHimalayan monasteries are important examples of Tibetan Buddhist cultural traditions."},
{"no":90,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Bijak is a compilation of the teachings of Kabir.\n2. Pushti Marg was founded by Madhvacharya.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Bhakti Movement","Dadu Dayal","Pushti Marg","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Bijak is an important collection associated with the teachings of Kabir.\nPushti Marg was founded by Vallabhacharya, not Madhvacharya.\nTherefore, only statement 1 is correct.","revisionPoints":"Bijak → Kabir\nPushti Marg → Vallabhacharya\nVallabhacharya was associated with the Shuddhadvaita tradition.\nKabir was an important figure of the Bhakti movement."},
{"no":91,"text":"The Manganiyars are particularly known for:","A":"A musical tradition of northwestern India","B":"Traditional metalwork of South India","C":"Buddhist monastic traditions","D":"Temple architecture of eastern India","ans":"A","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Music","tags":["Manganiyars","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"The Manganiyars are a hereditary musician community associated mainly with Rajasthan.\nThey are known for their rich traditional musical repertoire.\nTheir performances include folk and devotional traditions.","revisionPoints":"Manganiyars → Rajasthan\nKnown for traditional musical heritage.\nTheir music forms an important part of Rajasthan's cultural traditions.\nOften associated with hereditary musical transmission."},
{"no":92,"text":"Consider the following statements about Queen Victoria's Proclamation of 1858:\n1. It declared that the British Crown would not seek further territorial annexations in India.\n2. It transferred the administration of India from the East India Company to the British Crown.\n3. It sought to regulate the trade activities of the East India Company.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Queen Victoria’s Proclamation 1858","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"The Government of India Act, 1858 transferred the administration of India from the East India Company to the British Crown.\nQueen Victoria's Proclamation promised a policy of non-annexation and respect for Indian rulers.\nIt was not primarily intended to regulate the Company's trade activities.","revisionPoints":"1858 → Crown rule begins in India\nEast India Company's administrative role ended.\nSecretary of State for India became central to British Indian administration.\nQueen Victoria's Proclamation included assurances regarding:\nReligious non-interference\nRights of Indian princes\nEqual treatment under law"},
{"no":93,"text":"The Ibadat Khana was established by:","A":"Akbar","B":"Aurangzeb","C":"Shah Jahan","D":"Humayun","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Ibadat Khana","Fatehpur Sikri","Akbar","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Akbar established the Ibadat Khana at Fatehpur Sikri.\nIt was used for religious and philosophical discussions.\nScholars and representatives of different religious traditions participated in discussions.","revisionPoints":"Ibadat Khana → Akbar\nLocation → Fatehpur Sikri\nPurpose → religious/philosophical discussions.\nReflects Akbar's interest in interfaith dialogue."},
{"no":94,"text":"With reference to the Seed Replacement Rate (SRR) in Indian agriculture, consider the following statements:\n1. There is a large gap between demand and supply of quality seeds for low-value, high-volume crops.\n2. Seed Replacement Rate is not important for increasing agricultural productivity.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct according to the source?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Seed Replacement Rate","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"The source identifies a demand-supply gap in quality seeds, particularly for low-value and high-volume crops.\nSeed replacement is important because quality seeds can contribute to improved productivity.\nTherefore, statement 2 is incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Seed Replacement Rate → proportion of cropped area sown with quality/certified seed replacing farm-saved seed\nQuality seed can improve:\nYield\nUniformity\nDisease resistance\nAvailability of quality seed is an important agricultural constraint."},
{"no":95,"text":"With reference to Eco-Sensitive Zones (ESZs), consider the following statements:\n1. They are declared under the Wildlife Protection Act, 1972.\n2. They prohibit all human activities except agriculture.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Environmental Law","tags":["Eco-Sensitive Zones","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"According to the source, ESZs are not declared under the Wildlife Protection Act, 1972.\nThey also do not impose a blanket prohibition on all human activities.\nESZ regulations classify activities into categories such as prohibited, regulated and permissible.","revisionPoints":"ESZ → environmentally sensitive areas around protected areas\nDeclared under the Environment (Protection) Act, 1986.\nPurpose → reduce negative impacts of development around protected areas.\nNot all human activity is automatically prohibited."},
{"no":96,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. The National Tiger Conservation Authority is a statutory body.\n2. The National Ganga River Basin Authority was chaired by the Prime Minister.\n3. The Animal Welfare Board of India was established under the Environment (Protection) Act, 1986.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Environmental Law","tags":["Animal Welfare Board","National Tiger Conservation Authority","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"The National Tiger Conservation Authority (NTCA) is a statutory body.\nThe National Ganga River Basin Authority was chaired by the Prime Minister under the framework described in the source.\nThe Animal Welfare Board of India was not established under the Environment (Protection) Act, 1986.","revisionPoints":"NTCA → statutory body\nCreated under amendments to the Wildlife Protection Act.\nNational Ganga River Basin Authority was associated with Ganga conservation.\nThe Animal Welfare Board has a separate statutory basis."},
{"no":97,"text":"Which of the following pairs is/are correctly matched?\n1. Vitamin C — Scurvy\n2. Vitamin D — Rickets\n3. Vitamin E — Night blindness","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Vitamins","Deficiency Diseases","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Vitamin C deficiency → Scurvy.\nVitamin D deficiency → Rickets in children.\nNight blindness is primarily associated with Vitamin A deficiency, not Vitamin E deficiency.","revisionPoints":"Vitamin A → Night blindness\nVitamin C → Scurvy\nVitamin D → Rickets\nVitamin E → antioxidant functions\nVitamin deficiencies are frequently tested through disease–nutrient matching."},
{"no":98,"text":"What are the possible environmental effects of nanoparticles?\n1. They can accumulate in the environment.\n2. They can enter food chains.\n3. They can generate free radicals.\nWhich of the above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Materials Science","tags":["Nanoparticles","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Nanoparticles have extremely small dimensions and unique chemical properties.\nSome nanoparticles can persist or accumulate in environmental systems.\nThey may enter organisms and move through food chains.\nCertain nanoparticles can induce the formation of reactive oxygen species/free radicals.","revisionPoints":"Nanoparticles → extremely small particles\nTheir properties can differ significantly from bulk materials.\nPotential environmental concerns include:\nBioaccumulation\nFood-chain transfer\nOxidative stress\nEnvironmental effects depend on the type, size and chemical properties of the nanoparticle."},
{"no":99,"text":"The publication World Economic Outlook is released by:","A":"World Bank","B":"International Monetary Fund","C":"World Trade Organization","D":"Asian Development Bank","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"International Financial Institutions","tags":["World Economic Outlook","IMF","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"The World Economic Outlook (WEO) is a major publication of the International Monetary Fund (IMF).\nIt provides analysis and projections concerning the global economy.\nIt covers issues such as economic growth, inflation and macroeconomic developments.","revisionPoints":"World Economic Outlook → IMF\nIMF → international monetary cooperation and financial stability.\nWEO provides analysis of:\nGlobal growth\nInflation\nMacroeconomic trends\nEconomic risks\nDo not confuse WEO with World Bank development reports."},
{"no":100,"text":"Which of the following were classified as Non-Plan Expenditure in the earlier Indian budget classification?\n1. Defence expenditure\n2. Interest payments\n3. Salaries and pensions\n4. Subsidies","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1, 2, 3 and 4","D":"1, 3 and 4 only","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Public Finance","tags":["Non-Plan Expenditure","UPSC","2014"],"expl":"Under the earlier Plan/Non-Plan expenditure classification, several major government expenditures were classified as Non-Plan.\nThese included defence expenditure, interest payments, salaries and pensions, and subsidies.\nThe Plan/Non-Plan distinction was later discontinued as part of changes in India's budgetary framework.","revisionPoints":"Non-Plan expenditure → earlier budget classification\nMajor components included:\nDefence\nInterest payments\nSalaries\nPensions\nSubsidies\nIndia later discontinued the Plan/Non-Plan distinction.\nModern budget classification focuses more on revenue/capital expenditure and other functional categories."},
];
